.TH ODOX 1 "@DATE@" "odox @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
odox \- @SUMMARY@
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B odox
.I FILE
.RI [ ARGUMENT ...]
.br
.B odox
.B \-\-which
.I FILE
.SH DESCRIPTION
@DESCRIPTION@
.PP
The file decides. A text document opens in
.BR xodt (1),
a spreadsheet in
.BR xods (1),
a presentation in
.BR xodp (1).
The extension answers first, because it is free and it is the same answer the
desktop's own association gives; the package's declared media type answers where
the extension cannot, so a file named
.I download
or named nothing still opens in the right place.
.PP
The viewer is looked for beside this command and then on PATH, so a set
installed together finds its own siblings. Any further arguments are passed to
it. On this platform the command replaces itself with the viewer rather than
waiting for it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-which " FILE"
Print the name of the viewer that would open the file, and launch nothing.
.TP
.B \-h ", " \-\-help
A summary of this.
.TP
.B \-V ", " \-\-version
The version.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The viewer was started, or
.B \-\-which
answered.
.TP
.B 2
No file was given, the file is not there, or nothing here reads it.
.TP
.B 127
The viewer that reads the file is not installed.
.SH FILES
None. odox writes no configuration, no cache and no temporary copy.
.SH NOTES
The Debian package depends on all three viewers, so
.B apt install odox
installs the whole suite. Installed any other way — from crates.io, say — the
viewers are separate, and a file whose viewer is absent is reported as such
rather than opened.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR xodt (1),
.BR xods (1),
.BR xodp (1)
.SH AUTHOR
David M. Anderson.
